00:21:18.909 --> 00:21:19.559
- Ready?

00:21:19.559 --> 00:21:20.209
- Yeah.

00:21:20.209 --> 00:21:23.182
- So VaynerMedia has always been in like,

00:21:23.182 --> 00:21:24.250
turbo growth mode.
- [Gary] Aw, crap.

00:21:24.250 --> 00:21:24.946
- [Gary] Yes.

00:21:24.946 --> 00:21:25.550
- Right, like always.

00:21:25.550 --> 00:21:27.198
It's always bring on more clients,

00:21:27.198 --> 00:21:28.893
hire more people, run out of space,

00:21:28.893 --> 00:21:30.170
rinse and repeat, right?

00:21:30.170 --> 00:21:30.612
- Yes.

00:21:30.612 --> 00:21:32.376
- Do you think there will ever be a time

00:21:32.376 --> 00:21:35.195
where VaynerMedia prioritizes

00:21:37.383 --> 00:21:39.144
depth over width

00:21:39.144 --> 00:21:41.536
in terms of charging more, you know,

00:21:41.536 --> 00:21:42.488
bigger, fatter --

00:21:42.488 --> 00:21:43.672
- Scopes?

00:21:43.672 --> 00:21:45.065
- scopes, and --

00:21:45.065 --> 00:21:47.898
- Subjective call on the
quality of the output?

00:21:47.898 --> 00:21:51.915
- Right, or, but then that
laddering up to sort of...

00:21:54.956 --> 00:21:55.792
- A plateauing of people?

00:21:55.792 --> 00:21:58.578
- Turning into like big,
huge, lavish benefit packages

00:21:58.578 --> 00:22:00.879
and things like some agencies get

00:22:00.879 --> 00:22:02.973
really, really, really
crazy with that, right?

00:22:02.973 --> 00:22:04.287
Where we're scrappier, right?

00:22:04.287 --> 00:22:04.941
- Yup.

00:22:04.941 --> 00:22:05.939
- And I'm not saying that's the point --

00:22:05.939 --> 00:22:06.752
- No, no, no, you're not.

00:22:06.752 --> 00:22:09.352
- But do you think Vayner will ever

00:22:09.352 --> 00:22:10.092
slow down --
- [Gary] You mean like,

00:22:10.092 --> 00:22:11.555
paying people 200 thousand dollar bonues

00:22:11.555 --> 00:22:13.134
to be the best creative director
in the world kinda thing?

00:22:13.134 --> 00:22:14.295
- Well, yeah sure, I guess.

00:22:14.295 --> 00:22:15.943
- I mean, I'm just, I wanna
quantify that part of it.

00:22:15.943 --> 00:22:16.756
- Right, exactly.

00:22:16.756 --> 00:22:20.355
So it's like depth over
width in terms of like,

00:22:20.355 --> 00:22:22.491
sheer dollar amounts as
they relate to clients,

00:22:22.491 --> 00:22:24.790
employees, benefits, all that stuff.

00:22:24.790 --> 00:22:26.136
And I'm not saying it's bad right now,

00:22:26.136 --> 00:22:27.135
by any stretch, right?

00:22:27.135 --> 00:22:27.762
- Yeah, no.

00:22:27.762 --> 00:22:29.178
I'm not worried about that.

00:22:30.468 --> 00:22:32.071
So I think, for me,

00:22:32.071 --> 00:22:33.789
the way I run my business, which is why

00:22:33.789 --> 00:22:35.832
I think I'm running a much better business

00:22:35.832 --> 00:22:38.595
than all these other
agencies that do that,

00:22:38.595 --> 00:22:42.101
is because I think you can
achieve both in parallel.

00:22:42.101 --> 00:22:45.398
So I think what I uniquely
as an entrepreneur

00:22:45.398 --> 00:22:47.836
am good at, is running both trains.

00:22:47.836 --> 00:22:50.437
So I think you could easily, as,

00:22:50.437 --> 00:22:52.085
in the context of your question,

00:22:52.085 --> 00:22:55.197
speak to a very different level of quality

00:22:55.197 --> 00:22:58.122
in the building from 18 months ago.

00:22:58.122 --> 00:22:58.796
- Right.

00:22:58.796 --> 00:23:01.280
- So, if you just project out,

00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:02.766
if you think about the fact that

00:23:02.766 --> 00:23:05.808
we didn't have a single SVP
or real creative director

00:23:05.808 --> 00:23:08.942
like of any, real's not fair.

00:23:08.942 --> 00:23:11.102
A creative director of 10
years or more experience

00:23:11.102 --> 00:23:13.284
just 18 months ago.

00:23:13.284 --> 00:23:14.027
- Mmhmm.

00:23:14.027 --> 00:23:17.106
- That you can see that
happening in parallel.

00:23:17.106 --> 00:23:20.900
I think that, I think
where it gets disguised is

00:23:20.900 --> 00:23:24.999
we have people that come
from Gray and Ogilvy

00:23:24.999 --> 00:23:26.068
and things of that nature.

00:23:26.068 --> 00:23:27.716
We also have, what's
been very happy to me,

00:23:27.716 --> 00:23:31.036
to see Aton and Harry and Pensoot

00:23:31.036 --> 00:23:34.116
and all these people
leveling up in parallel.

00:23:34.116 --> 00:23:36.118
What it gets disguised by is the thing

00:23:36.118 --> 00:23:37.441
that's much more obvious.

00:23:37.441 --> 00:23:40.901
What's much more obvious is
hyper growth of people, right?

00:23:40.901 --> 00:23:41.598
- Yeah.

00:23:41.598 --> 00:23:43.734
- It's just, getting
crammed when we grow, right?

00:23:43.734 --> 00:23:46.888
What's less obvious is,
you know, anyone's ability

00:23:46.888 --> 00:23:51.346
to really truly dissect 550
people's depth of skill.

00:23:51.346 --> 00:23:51.876
- Yeah.

00:23:51.876 --> 00:23:54.438
- And there's pockets of clients.

00:23:54.438 --> 00:23:56.288
You're always gonna have
variable depending on the people.

00:23:56.288 --> 00:23:57.774
Plus, hiring the right people.

00:23:57.774 --> 00:24:01.350
So, to me, the answer is,
it's happening in parallel.

00:24:02.855 --> 00:24:04.225
I think the only thing that makes people

00:24:04.225 --> 00:24:07.615
think that it's happening,
is stop the hiring process

00:24:07.615 --> 00:24:09.705
and then they're like, "Oh!

00:24:09.705 --> 00:24:13.838
"We're not going hyper,
we're going deeper."

00:24:13.838 --> 00:24:14.441
- Yeah.

00:24:14.441 --> 00:24:16.554
- I think we've gone stunningly deep

00:24:16.554 --> 00:24:18.853
in 18 months by two variables.

00:24:18.853 --> 00:24:21.778
One, bringing the outside
talent in their 30s and 40s,

00:24:21.778 --> 00:24:25.285
right, depth, which is gray hairs.

00:24:25.285 --> 00:24:28.303
in a lot of people's
subjective point of view.

00:24:28.303 --> 00:24:30.695
And number two, the actual growth

00:24:30.695 --> 00:24:32.111
of the people that have been at Vayner

00:24:32.111 --> 00:24:33.550
that are totally different animals.

00:24:33.550 --> 00:24:37.637
And I think that, for every
individual it's different.

00:24:37.637 --> 00:24:39.587
And I also think that for the people

00:24:39.587 --> 00:24:41.329
that have been here longer,

00:24:41.329 --> 00:24:45.230
it's a different context point
than it is for other people.

00:24:45.230 --> 00:24:48.451
You know, and so, no I mean, I think

00:24:48.451 --> 00:24:49.936
as long as I'm running the business,

00:24:49.936 --> 00:24:53.094
I'm never comfortable in thinking

00:24:53.094 --> 00:24:55.787
one has to be done without the other.

00:24:55.787 --> 00:24:58.063
I truly feel that you're
capable to do both.

00:24:58.063 --> 00:25:00.547
And I think if you project
out what's happened

00:25:00.547 --> 00:25:02.637
in the last 18 months on the depth chart,

00:25:02.637 --> 00:25:06.236
that it's actually tremendously scalable

00:25:06.236 --> 00:25:08.094
and gets way deeper
because as you get bigger,

00:25:08.094 --> 00:25:09.858
you deploy those dollars to those things.

00:25:09.858 --> 00:25:10.624
- Mmhmm.

00:25:10.624 --> 00:25:14.041
- And as far as like, benefits
and packages, you know.

00:25:14.041 --> 00:25:17.222
I'm very weird when it comes to bonuses.

00:25:17.222 --> 00:25:19.846
I think bonuses, so we've
hired two people recently

00:25:19.846 --> 00:25:22.214
that left their agencies
because of bonuses.

00:25:22.214 --> 00:25:24.397
Because they felt they deserved more.

00:25:24.397 --> 00:25:27.299
I think when you have a graying zone of,

00:25:27.299 --> 00:25:30.364
I get to be the judge
and jury on the bonus,

00:25:30.364 --> 00:25:33.909
I think what is safer
is to just try to get

00:25:33.909 --> 00:25:35.790
to a number together.

00:25:35.790 --> 00:25:38.530
And I think that those
things ebb and flow.

00:25:38.530 --> 00:25:41.618
I also think that when you're
in a fast-growing company,

00:25:41.618 --> 00:25:44.474
what a lot of employees don't calibrate,

00:25:44.474 --> 00:25:47.168
'cause they shouldn't,
'cause it's very hard

00:25:47.168 --> 00:25:48.445
for anybody to care about anything

00:25:48.445 --> 00:25:50.232
besides what they care
about for themselves,

00:25:50.232 --> 00:25:52.369
is the notion of what happens

00:25:52.369 --> 00:25:55.503
in a 36-month window
versus a 12-month window.

00:25:55.503 --> 00:25:58.197
So I've been happy to be honest with you,

00:25:58.197 --> 00:26:01.006
of getting three to four
emails in the last six months

00:26:01.006 --> 00:26:03.676
from former employees
who left because of money

00:26:03.676 --> 00:26:05.789
who now make less money than some

00:26:05.789 --> 00:26:09.574
of their counterparts here
because VaynerMedia is growing,

00:26:09.574 --> 00:26:12.801
and we've been able to rise all ships,

00:26:12.801 --> 00:26:15.170
and in their other places they haven't.

00:26:15.170 --> 00:26:17.236
And so, those are my answers.

00:26:17.236 --> 00:26:17.979
- Bonus question.

00:26:17.979 --> 00:26:18.606
- Please.

00:26:18.606 --> 00:26:21.880
- How fast do you think
we max out the space

00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:22.994
at Hudson Yards?

00:26:22.994 --> 00:26:24.573
- I think we might of already.

00:26:24.573 --> 00:26:25.223
- Nice.

00:26:25.223 --> 00:26:28.242
I think, you know, I do think that

00:26:31.678 --> 00:26:34.720
every company has to bear the negatives

00:26:34.720 --> 00:26:36.554
that come along with the positives

00:26:36.554 --> 00:26:38.923
for their individual
self around the person

00:26:38.923 --> 00:26:40.223
that runs the company.

00:26:40.223 --> 00:26:42.363
I love insanity.

00:26:42.363 --> 00:26:43.383
(laughter)

00:26:43.383 --> 00:26:44.213
I like it.

00:26:44.213 --> 00:26:45.165
I like when we're like this.

00:26:45.165 --> 00:26:47.115
I like when we're sitting
this close to each other.

00:26:47.115 --> 00:26:47.975
I like it.

00:26:47.975 --> 00:26:50.157
And if you are not that,

00:26:50.157 --> 00:26:51.690
if you're somebody that really like that,

00:26:51.690 --> 00:26:53.710
and your space, and this
is where I put this,

00:26:53.710 --> 00:26:55.172
and this is where I put this,

00:26:55.172 --> 00:26:58.423
then, you know, Vayner
can be tricky at times.

00:27:00.413 --> 00:27:02.974
Though, no question, I
think it's a leader's job

00:27:02.974 --> 00:27:05.946
to adjust to his reality,
and I've definitely

00:27:05.946 --> 00:27:08.802
deployed more empathy towards the way

00:27:08.802 --> 00:27:10.497
we plan on scaling Hudson Yards,

00:27:10.497 --> 00:27:13.113
and if we need secondary offices to try

00:27:13.113 --> 00:27:17.548
to come back a little bit
on my own selfish love

00:27:17.548 --> 00:27:18.964
of that kinetic energy.

00:27:18.964 --> 00:27:22.447
Mainly predicated on, because
the floors are so big.

00:27:22.447 --> 00:27:25.512
So I think I'm gonna be
able to scratch my itch,

00:27:25.512 --> 00:27:27.718
'cause they're just big floor plates

00:27:27.718 --> 00:27:29.942
and there'll be three, four
hundred people comfortably

00:27:29.942 --> 00:27:31.173
on one floor.

00:27:31.173 --> 00:27:34.052
And so I'm hoping that
solves that problem.

00:27:35.092 --> 00:27:37.460
So, thanks Steve.

00:27:38.417 --> 00:27:40.159
All right, that's all been one,

00:27:40.159 --> 00:27:43.360
this whole episode's been
one big, kind of like,

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:45.287
trying to stretch it
out as long as possible

00:27:45.287 --> 00:27:47.237
so I can really figure
out, what was happening

00:27:47.237 --> 00:27:49.745
in my other parallel
mind, during this show,

00:27:49.745 --> 00:27:51.208
just like I run businesses, in parallel.

00:27:51.208 --> 00:27:52.972
In my other parallel mind, which is like,

00:27:52.972 --> 00:27:54.528
what is really gonna happen on Sunday

00:27:54.528 --> 00:27:55.921
between the Jets and the Raiders?

00:27:55.921 --> 00:27:58.754
You know, I can't lie, I
was affected last night

00:27:58.754 --> 00:28:01.262
by the Patriots dismantling the Dolphins.

00:28:01.262 --> 00:28:04.025
And me projecting then
just to four days earlier

00:28:04.025 --> 00:28:05.882
the Jets hung in there pretty tight.

00:28:05.882 --> 00:28:07.299
I've been really worried about this.

00:28:07.299 --> 00:28:09.575
I think the Raiders
have the kind of offense

00:28:09.575 --> 00:28:11.942
that could pose a little
bit of trouble for the Jets.

00:28:11.942 --> 00:28:14.821
I also thought the Jets played
particularly well last week.

00:28:15.839 --> 00:28:18.974
And I do think Oakland is a
very difficult place to play.

00:28:18.974 --> 00:28:21.528
They're coming off a huge
win against the Chargers,

00:28:21.528 --> 00:28:23.126
a rival of theirs on the road.

00:28:23.126 --> 00:28:25.657
That place is gonna be
rowdy and uncomfortable.

00:28:26.967 --> 00:28:28.440
Though the Jets have
shown enormous composure

00:28:28.440 --> 00:28:30.669
this year with Todd Bowles at the helm

00:28:30.669 --> 00:28:32.666
and have swung me.

00:28:32.666 --> 00:28:35.730
I've been picking the
Raiders in my head all week

00:28:35.730 --> 00:28:39.771
right up to this point,
and in the moment of truth,

00:28:42.141 --> 00:28:45.645
in the moment of truth, I'm
gonna go 25-24 New York Jets

00:28:45.645 --> 00:28:49.473
in an absolute nail biter, nervous scary.

00:28:49.473 --> 00:28:51.121
(shudders and buzzes)

